    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_128",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When transposing a G major melody written in bass clef, which of the following transpositions does not require changing the position of the notes?\n\nOptions: \nA. Keep the clef the same, transpose to a minor second above\nB. Transpose to F major, using the alto clef\nC. Transpose to D major, using the treble clef\nD. Transpose to a major second below, using the treble clef\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which transposition does not require changing the position of the notes. This means the notes in the new clef and key should occupy the same vertical space on the staff as the original notes in the bass clef.\n\nLet's analyze the original melody: G major in bass clef.\nThe G major scale in bass clef is G, A, B, C, D, E, F#, G.\nThe notes of the G major scale in bass clef are located on the following lines and spaces:\nG: 3rd space from the top\nA: 2nd line from the top\nB: 1st line from the top\nC: Top space\nD: Top line\nE: 1st space from the bottom\nF#: 1st line from the bottom\n\nNow let's consider each option:\n\nA. Keep the clef the same (bass clef), transpose to a minor second above.\nA minor second above G is G#. This means we are now in G# major, which is not a common key. Even if we consider a minor second transposition of the G major scale itself, the resulting scale would be unusual. More importantly, transposing a minor second above in bass clef would move all notes up by one semitone, which inherently changes their position on the staff. For example, G would become G#.\n\nB. Transpose to F major, using the alto clef.\nF major has one flat (Bb). The alto clef places middle C on the third line from the bottom.\nIn alto clef, the notes corresponding to the lines and spaces are:\n1st space: D\n1st line: E\n2nd space: F\n2nd line: G\n3rd space: A\n3rd line: C\n4th space: B\n4th line: D\n5th space: E\n5th line: F\n\nLet's see if the F major scale in alto clef aligns with the positions of the original G major scale in bass clef.\nThe F major scale is F, G, A, Bb, C, D, E, F.\nF: 2nd space\nG: 2nd line\nA: 3rd space\nBb: 3rd line (if we consider Bb, which is not in G major)\nC: 4th space (middle C)\nD: 4th line\nE: 5th space\n\nComparing the positions of G major in bass clef with F major in alto clef:\nOriginal G in bass clef is on the 3rd space from the top.\nIn alto clef, G is on the 2nd line. These are different positions.\n\nC. Transpose to D major, using the treble clef.\nD major has two sharps (F# and C#). The treble clef places G on the second line from the bottom.\nIn treble clef, the notes corresponding to the lines and spaces are:\n1st space: F\n1st line: E\n2nd space: D\n2nd line: G\n3rd space: B\n3rd line: A\n4th space: C\n4th line: F\n5th space: E\n5th line: G\n\nLet's see if the D major scale in treble clef aligns with the positions of the original G major scale in bass clef.\nThe D major scale is D, E, F#, G, A, B, C#, D.\nD: 2nd space\nE: 1st line\nF#: 1st space (F) + sharp\nG: 2nd line\nA: 3rd line\nB: 3rd space\nC#: 4th space (C) + sharp\n\nLet's re-examine the question. \"does not require changing the position of the notes\". This implies that if a note was on a certain line or in a certain space in the bass clef, the corresponding note in the new key and clef should be on the same line or space.\n\nConsider the notes of the G major scale in bass clef and their positions:\nG (3rd space from top)\nA (2nd line from top)\nB (1st line from top)\nC (top space)\nD (top line)\nE (1st space from bottom)\nF# (1st line from bottom)\n\nLet's consider a common perfect fifth transposition up, which is a very common transposition. If we transpose G major up a perfect fifth, we get D major.\nIf we were to transpose G major written in bass clef, up a perfect fifth while staying in bass clef, the notes would become D, E, F#, G, A, B, C#, D. The positions would change significantly because the keynote has changed.\n\nLet's reinterpret \"does not require changing the position of the notes\". This could mean that the *relative* position of the notes on the staff should be maintained, meaning if a G was on the 3rd space from the top, then the new tonic (or some significant note) should also be on the 3rd space from the top.\n\nLet's re-evaluate option C more carefully. Transpose to D major, using the treble clef.\nOriginal: G major in bass clef.\nNotes of G major scale in bass cl