    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_264",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After converting notes like B-♯f to enharmonic equivalents, which of the following correctly maintains the same interval group?\n\nOptions: \nA. do-♭sol as a diminished fifth\nB. ♭c-♭g as a perfect fifth\nC. ♮A-♯e as a perfect fifth\nD. ♭C-♭G as a perfect fifth\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze B-♯f first.\nB to F♯ is an augmented fifth.\nEnharmonic equivalence means spelling the same pitch with a different name.\nSo, B-♯f can be spelled enharmonically as C♭-F♯, C♭-G♭, C-F♯, or C-G♭, etc.\n\nNow let's examine the options to find the one that maintains the same interval group when considering enharmonic equivalents.\n\nA. do-♭sol as a diminished fifth.\n\"do\" usually refers to C. So C-♭G.\nC to G♭ is a diminished fifth.\nIf we consider the interval group as diminished fifth, we need to see if B-♯f enharmonically can lead to a diminished fifth. B-♯f is an augmented fifth. An augmented fifth and a diminished sixth are enharmonic. A diminished fifth and an augmented fourth are enharmonic.\n\nConsider the interval B to F♯. This interval is a major sixth.\nB to F is a diminished fifth.\nB to F♯ is a major sixth by spelling.\nLet's re-evaluate B-♯f. B is a note. ♯f is an F sharp. The interval from B up to F is a diminished fifth (B-C-D-E-F is 5 notes). B to F natural is a diminished fifth. B to F sharp is an augmented fifth.\n\nLet's assume the question meant \"B to F-sharp\". This is an augmented fifth.\nLet's assume it meant \"B-flat to F-sharp\". This is an augmented fifth.\nLet's assume it meant \"B to F\". This is a diminished fifth.\n\nThe question uses \"notes like B-♯f\". This is a bit ambiguous. Let's assume it means the interval from B to F♯. This is an augmented fifth, as there are 5 letter names (B, C, D, E, F) and the distance from B to F is perfect, so from B to F♯ is augmented.\n\nLet's re-examine the question with the common understanding of enharmonic equivalents and interval groups. The term \"interval group\" can refer to the size and quality of the interval. We are looking for an option that, after potential enharmonic spelling, represents an interval that is enharmonically equivalent to B-♯f.\n\nLet's assume B-♯f refers to the interval spelled as B to F♯. This is an **augmented fifth**.\nTwo intervals