ted fifth.\n\nA. do-♭sol as a diminished fifth.\nC to G♭. C to G is a perfect fifth (7 semitones). G♭ is lower, so C to G♭ is a diminished fifth (6 semitones). This is not 8 semitones.\n\nB. ♭c-♭g as a perfect fifth.\nC♭ to G♭. As analyzed before, this is a diminished sixth (8 semitones). The option incorrectly states it's a perfect fifth. However, the semitone count matches.\n\nC. ♮A-♯e as a perfect fifth.\nA to E. A to E is a perfect fifth (7 semitones). A to E♯ is an augmented fifth (8 semitones).\nHere, A to E♯ is an augmented fifth.\nLet's consider A to E♯. A to E is a perfect fifth. E♯ is one semitone sharper than E. So A to E♯ is an augmented fifth. This has 8 semitones.\nThis is enharmonically equivalent to the original B-♯f (augmented fifth).\nThe option states \"as a perfect fifth\". This is incorrect spelling; it's an augmented fifth.\n\nD. ♭C-♭G as a perfect fifth.\nC♭ to G♭. This is the same as option B. It's a diminished sixth (8 semitones). The option states \"as a perfect fifth\", which is incorrect.\n\nThere seems to be an issue with the phrasing of the options, as they all incorrectly label the interval quality. However, we are looking for the *interval group* that maintains the same *interval group*. The interval group is defined by the number of semitones.\n\nLet's assume B-♯f refers to the interval B to F natural. This is a diminished fifth (6 semitones).\nLet's recheck the options for 6 semitones.\nA. C to G♭ is a diminished fifth (6 semitones).\nB. C♭ to G♭ is a diminished sixth (8 semitones).\nC. A to E♯ is an augmented fifth (8 semitones).\nD. C♭ to G♭ is a diminished sixth (8 semitones).\n\nIf B-♯f refers to the diminished fifth, then option A is the correct answer, assuming \"do\" is C. However, the use of \"♯f\" suggests F sharp.\n\nLet's assume B-♯f means the interval B to F♯, which is an augmented fifth (8 semitones). We need an option that has 8 semitones and is enharmonically related.\n\nLet's re-examine option C: ♮A-♯e as a perfect f