nted fifth.\nOption C, A-♯e, is also an augmented fifth. Both have the same name and semitone count.\nOptions B and D, C♭-G♭, are diminished sixths, which are *enharmonically equivalent* to augmented fifths in terms of semitone count, but are different interval groups in typical classification.\n\nHowever, the question asks to convert \"notes like B-♯f to enharmonic equivalents\". This suggests that we might be starting with the spelled interval B-♯f and converting its notes to enharmonic equivalents.\n\nLet's re-interpret the question in a different way. The \"interval group\" might refer to the set of semitones spanned by the interval.\nB to F♯ is 8 semitones.\n\nOption C: A to E♯ is 8 semitones.\nThis option correctly identifies the interval \"A\" to \"E♯\". The interval from A to E is a perfect fifth. Raising E by a semitone (to E♯) makes it an augmented fifth. So, A to E♯ is an augmented fifth.\nThe option says \"as a perfect fifth\", which is incorrect *spelling*. However, the interval group (augmented fifth) has the same semitone count (8 semitones) as the interval group of B to F♯ (augmented fifth).\n\nLet's analyze option B and D again: ♭c-♭g. C♭ to G♭. The interval from C to G is a perfect fifth. G♭ is one semitone lower than G. So C to G♭ is diminished fifth. C♭ to G♭: C♭ is one semitone lower than C. So C♭ to G♭ is a diminished sixth. (8 semitones).\n\nBoth B and C (and D are duplicate of B regarding the notes) have the same semitone count as B-♯f.\nThe question asks which *correctly maintains the same interval group*. This implies that the resulting interval should be the same type or enharmonically equivalent.\n\nIf B-♯f refers to the augmented fifth, then A-♯e is also an augmented fifth. This is a direct match.\nThe phrasing \"correctly maintains the same interval group\" suggests that the interval itself, or its enharmonic equivalent.\n\nLet's consider the most direct interpretation: B-♯f is an augmented fifth. We are looking for another augmented fifth or a diminished sixth.\nOption C is A-♯e, which is an augmented fifth.\nOptions B and D are C♭-G♭, which is a diminished sixth.\nBoth are valid in terms of semitone count.\n\nHowever, if a question asks to maintain the \"same interval group\", and the original is an augmented fifth, then finding another augmented fifth is more direct than finding a diminished sixth. The fact that the options have incorrect \"as a perfect fifth\" labels is confusing.\n\nLet's assume the question implies that the notes themselves are to be converted enharmonically.\nIf we are given the interval B-♯f (an augmented fifth).\nOptions give spelled intervals. We need to find the one that, when interpreted as an interval, has the same semitone count.\n\nB-♯f = 8 semitones.\nA. do-♭sol. C to G♭ = 6 semitones.\nB. ♭c-♭g. C♭ to G♭ = 8 semitones.\nC. ♮A-♯e. A to E♯ = 8 semitones.\nD. ♭C-♭G. C♭ to G♭ = 8 semitones.\n\nBoth B, C, and D have the same number of semitones.\nThe question is which one *correctly maintains the same interval group*.\n\nLet's consider the context of \"converting notes like B-♯f to enharmonic equivalents\". This implies we might start with B-♯f and change the notes to something enharmonically equivalent.\nOr, we are looking for an interval that is enharmonically equivalent to B-♯f.\n\nIf we consider the \"interval group\" to be the set of semitones. Then B, C, D are all valid in semitone count.\nHowever, the phrasing \"correctly maintains\" suggests a more specific relationship.\n\nLet's assume B-♯f is intended to be an augmented fifth.\nOption C: ♮A-♯e. This interval is indeed an augmented fifth. So, it's the same interval group in terms of quality and size.\n\nLet's think about enharmonic spelling. B can be spelled C♭. F♯ can be spelled G♭. So, C♭-G♭ would be an enharmonic equivalent in terms of notes. C♭-G♭ is a diminished sixth (8 semitones).\n\nConsider option C: ♮A-♯e. This is an augmented fifth.\nThe original B-♯f (B to F♯) is an augmented fifth.\nThey are the s