a unison. The interval from B to B# is a major second. The interval from B to Bb is a minor second. So the interval from Bb to B# is a diminished second. A diminished second is enharmonically equivalent to a major third (e.g., C to E). Major thirds are consonant. The spelling uses accidentals, so it's altered. Thus, B fits.\n\nD. ♯♯D - C. The interval from D to C is a major seventh descending. So ascending D to C is a major seventh descending, which is a minor second ascending. No, D to C is a major seventh descending. Ascending interval is from D to C. D to C an octave below is D. D down to C is a major seventh. The question is ascending, notated as ♯♯D - C. Let's assume D is the lower note. D to D is an octave. D down to C is a major seventh. So from D to C, it's descending. If it is ascending from D to C, it's a major seventh. With ♯♯D, it is E. So E to C. E to C is a major sixth. Major sixths are consonant. The spelling involves accidentals, so it is altered. Thus, D fits.\n\nTherefore, B and D are both consonant (enharmonically or in their basic numerical form after resolving accidentals) and altered. Hence, the answer is BD.\n\nFinal check of options. BD is an option.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
