    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4767",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A more accurate calibration curve and conversion for radiocarbon dating can be derived from:\n\nOptions: \nA. coins recovered from ancient shipwrecks.\nB. dendrochronology.\nC. all of the above.\nD. ice core sampling.\nE. written records.\nF. bone dating of ancient human remains.\nG. examination of sediment layers.\nH. satellite imagery.\nI. fossil records.\nJ. carbon dating of historical buildings.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **B. dendrochronology.**\n\nDendrochronology, or tree-ring dating, provides an annual record of tree growth, and thus has a precise calendar age. By dating wood samples with known dendrochronological ages using radiocarbon dating, scientists can create a calibration curve that corrects for variations in atmospheric carbon-14 levels over time, leading to more accurate radiocarbon dates. The other options either do not provide the necessary precision for calibration (like ice core sampling or sediment layers) or are themselves subject to the same atmospheric variations that radiocarbon dating needs to correct for (like bone or building dating).\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 157,
        "output_tokens": 141,
