    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4482",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"Since the foundations of the American commonwealth were laid in colonial times over 300 years ago, vigorous complaint and more or less bitter persecution have been aimed at newcomers to our shores. Also the congressional reports of about 1840 are full of abuse of English, Scotch, Welsh immigrants as paupers, criminals, and so forth. Old citizens in Detroit of Irish and German descent have told me of the fierce tirades and propaganda directed against the great waves of Irish and Germans who came over from 1840 on for a few decades to escape civil, racial, and religious persecution in their native lands. The \"Know-Nothings,\" lineal ancestors of the Ku-Klux Klan, bitterly denounced the Irish and Germans as mongrels, scum, foreigners, and a menace to our institutions, much as other great branches of the Caucasian race of glorious history and antecedents are berated to-day….But to-day it is the Italians, Spanish, Poles, Jews, Greeks, Russians, Balkanians, and so forth, who are the racial lepers…..In this bill we find racial discrimination at its worst—a deliberate attempt to go back 84 years in our census taken every 10 years so that a blow may be aimed at peoples of eastern and southern Europe, particularly at our recent allies in the Great War—Poland and Italy.\"\nRobert H. Clancy, Congressional Record, 68th Congress, 1st Session, 1924\nWhich one of the following legislative acts is most closely an example of nativism?\n\nOptions: \nA. The Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965\nB. The War Powers Act of 1973\nC. The Homestead Act of 1862\nD. The Social Security Act of 1935\nE. The Naturalization Act of 1790\nF. The Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882\nG. The Espionage Act of 1917\nH. The Wagner Act of 1935\nI. The Civil Rights Act of 1964\nJ. The Alien and Sedition Acts of 1798\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The provided text discusses historical and contemporary instances of discrimination against immigrants in the United States, highlighting how different groups have been scapegoated and marginalized. It emphasizes a pattern of \"bitter persecution aimed at newcomers\" and racial discrimination. The question asks which legislative act is the most closely an example of nativism, which is the policy of protecting the interests of native-born or established inhabitants against those of immigrants.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n\n*   **A. The Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965:** This act abolished the national origins quota system and is generally seen as a move towards more equitable immigration policies, not nativism.\n*   **B. The War Powers Act of 1973:** This act relates to the powers of the President in conducting military operations and has nothing to do with immigration or nativism.\n*   **C. The Homestead Act of 1862:** This act encouraged westward expansion by providing settlers with federal land. While it had implications for settlement patterns, it was not primarily focused on anti-immigrant sentiment.\n*   **D. The Social Security Act of 1935:** This act established a system of old-age benefits, unemployment insurance, and aid to dependent children. It is unrelated to immigration policy or nativism.\n*   **E. The Naturalization Act of 1790:** This act established the process for naturalizing immigrants but also restricted it to \"free white persons,\" which shows an early form of nativism by excluding non-white immigrants.\n*   **F. The Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882:** This act overtly prohibited the immigration of all Chinese laborers, marking a significant piece of legislation explicitly driven by anti-immigrant sentiment and xenophobia, the core of nativism.\n*   **G. The Espionage Act of 1917:** This act restricted speech and activities that interfered with the U.S. war effort and is unrelated to immigration policy.\n*   **H. The Wagner Act of 1935:** Also known as the National Labor Relations Act, this act protected the rights of workers to organize and bargain collectively. It is unrelated to immigration policy.\n*   **I. The Civil Rights Act of 1964:** This act outlawed discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex, or national origin and is the opposite of nativism.\n*   **J. The Alien and Sedition Acts of 1798:** These acts strengthened federal powers to deport foreigners and restricted speech critical of the government, showing an anti-immigrant and anti-dissident stance, which aligns with nativist sentiments.\n\nComparing the options, both the Naturalization Act of 1790 and the Alien and Sedition Acts of 1798 show elements of nativism. However, the **Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882** is a much more prominent and direct legislative example of excluding an entire nationality based on xenophobic and nativist motivations, fitting the description of \"racial discrimination at its worst\" and \"a menace to our institutions\" mentioned in the provided text's historical context. The text also criticizes legislation that aims \"to go back 84 years in our census\" in 1924, which directly points to the quota system that was heavily influenced by earlier nativist legislation like the Chinese Exclusion Act.\n\nThe Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882 is a direct legislative manifestation of nativist sentiment, explicitly targeting and prohibiting the immigration of a specific ethnic group. This aligns with the historical pattern of hostility towards newcomers described in the provided text. While other acts might have elements of nativism, the Chinese Exclusion Act is undeniably one of the most prominent and stark examples in U.S. history.\n\nWhile the Naturalization Act of 1790 established an early discriminatory framework and the Alien and Sedition Acts aimed at control and exclusion, the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882 represents a more direct and comprehensive legislative act of outright exclusion driven by nativism.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 521,
        "output_tokens": 854,
        "total_tokens": 1375
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4483",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The law of love, peace and liberty in the states extending to Jews, Turks and Egyptians, as they are considered sonnes of Adam, which is the glory of the outward state of Holland, soe love, peace and liberty, extending to all in Christ Jesus, condemns hatred, war and bondage. And because our Saviour sayeth it is impossible but that offences will come, but woe unto him by whom they cometh, our desire is not to offend one of his little ones, in whatsoever form, name or title hee appears in, whether Presbyterian, Independent, Baptist or Quaker, but shall be glad to see anything of God in any of them, desiring to doe unto all men as we desire all men should doe unto us, which is the true law both of Church and State; for our Saviour sayeth this is the law and the prophets.\n\"Therefore if any of these said persons come in love unto us, we cannot in conscience lay violent hands upon them, but give them free egresse and regresse unto our Town, and houses, as God shall persuade our consciences, for we are bounde by the law of God and man to doe good unto all men and evil to noe man. And this is according to the patent and charter of our Towne, given unto us in the name of the States General, which we are not willing to infringe, and violate, but shall houlde to our patent and shall remaine, your humble subjects, the inhabitants of Vlishing (Flushing, part of the colony of New Netherlands).\"\n—The Flushing Remonstrance, 1657\nWhich of the following was most significant in enshrining into the U.S. legal structure the ideas contained in the Flushing Remonstrance?\n\nOptions: \nA. The enumeration of congressional powers in the Constitution.\nB. The \"Right to Bear Arms\" clause of the Second Amendment.\nC. The 14th Amendment's \"Equal Protection Clause\"\nD. The \"Due Process Clause\" of the Fifth Amendment.\nE. The \"Right to a Speedy and Public Trial\" clause of the Sixth Amendment.\nF. The \"Cruel and Unusual Punishment\" clause of the Eighth Amendment.\nG. The \"Establishment Clause\" of the First Amendment.\nH. The \"Free Exercise Clause\" of the First Amendment.\nI. The preamble of the Declaration of Independence.\nJ. The \"Double Jeopardy\" clause of the Fifth Amendment.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o-mini",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{G}. The \"Establishment Clause\" of the First Amendment aligns with the ideas of love, peace, and liberty mentioned in the Flushing Remonstrance, particularly in its emphasis on freedom of religion and the separation of church and state, thus enshrining those principles into U.S. law.",
