  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4496",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nThe following quote is from Voltaire in response to the 1755 Lisbon earthquake.\nMy dear sir, nature is very cruel. One would find it hard to imagine how the laws of movement cause such frightful disasters in the best of possible worlds. A hundred thousand ants, our fellows, crushed all at once in our ant-hill, and half of them perishing, no doubt in unspeakable agony, beneath the wreckage from which they cannot be drawn. Families ruined all over Europe, the fortune of a hundred businessmen, your compatriots, swallowed up in the ruins of Lisbon. What a wretched gamble is the game of human life! What will the preachers say, especially if the palace of the Inquisition is still standing? I flatter myself that at least the reverend father inquisitors have been crushed like others. That ought to teach men not to persecute each other, for while a few holy scoundrels burn a few fanatics, the earth swallows up one and all.\n—Voltaire, in a letter, 1755\nWhich of the following Enlightenment philosophes questioned reliance on reason and began the Romantic Movement for the rationale given?\n\nOptions: \nA. Thomas Hobbes because he thought human nature was naturally cruel and selfish.\nB. Jean-Jacques Rousseau because he thought society corrupted noble souls.\nC. Mary Wollstonecraft because she railed against the subjugation of women emotionally.\nD. Denis Diderot because he believed emotions were superior to reason.\nE. John Locke because he thought individuals were corrupted by power.\nF. Immanuel Kant because he thought that reason was insufficient to explain the mysteries of the universe.\nG. Adam Smith because he stated that reason could not be used to understand how another feels.\nH. Voltaire himself because he criticized the cruelty of nature and human suffering.\nI. Baron Montesquieu because he thought that governments were not capable of being fair.\nJ. David Hume because he believed that reason was inadequate to make moral judgments.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which Enlightenment philosophe questioned reliance on reason and began the Romantic Movement for the rationale given in the quote. The quote expresses anguish over natural disasters and human suffering, questioning the idea of a \"best of all possible worlds\" and highlighting the cruelty of nature and human persecution. This sentiment, emphasizing emotion and the limitations of reason when faced with the sublime and the tragic, aligns with the nascent stages of Romanticism.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n\nA. Thomas Hobbes: Hobbes believed in the inherent cruelty and selfishness of human nature, but his philosophy is more associated with the foundations of political philosophy and social contract theory, not necessarily the beginnings of Romanticism or questioning of reason in the context of this quote.\n\nB. Jean-Jacques Rousseau: Rousseau is a key figure in questioning the effects of society and emphasizing the natural goodness of humanity. His ideas about the corruption of the \"noble savage\" by society resonate with a critique of civilization and a focus on natural sentiment, which are precursors to Romanticism. He also emphasized emotions and the limitations of purely rational thought.\n\nC. Mary Wollstonecraft: Wollstonecraft championed women's rights and education, focusing on rational equality and challenging societal injustices. While her work was groundbreaking, the quote's primary focus is on existential suffering and the limitations of reason in understanding nature's cruelty, rather than women's subjugation.\n\nD. Denis Diderot: Diderot was a prominent figure of the Enlightenment and the editor of the Encyclopédie, generally championing reason and empirical observation. While he explored human emotions, his primary orientation was within the Enlightenment's rational framework.\n\nE. John Locke: Locke was a foundational Enlightenment thinker who emphasized reason, empiricism, and natural rights. His focus was on how individuals acquired knowledge and the establishment of just governance, not on questioning reason's sufficiency in the face of existential suffering.\n\nF. Immanuel Kant: Kant, though grappling with reason's limits and the sublime, is more associated with the later Enlightenment and the development of critical philosophy. While his exploration of the limits of reason is relevant, Rousseau's earlier emphasis on emotion and the critique of artificial society are more directly linked to the genesis of Romanticism and the sentiment of the Voltaire quote.\n\nG. Adam Smith: Smith, primarily an economist, focused on reason in the context of economic systems and human self-interest. His work doesn't directly address the existential questioning of reason presented in the quote or the origins of Romanticism.\n\nH. Voltaire himself: While Voltaire expresses profound shock and anguish, and his criticism of suffering and superstition can be seen as pushing boundaries, the question asks which *other* philosophe questioned reliance on reason and *began* the Romantic Movement. Voltaire, while sympathetic to the feelings expressed, remains largely within the Enlightenment's critical and rational tradition.\n\nI. Baron Montesquieu: Montesquieu focused on the separation of powers and the analysis of different forms of government. His contribution is primarily in political theory, not in questioning reason's overall reliance or initiating Romanticism.\n\nJ. David Hume: Hume famously questioned the certainty of reason, particularly in matters of causality and morality, leaning towards empiricism and skepticism. However, his skepticism, while related to the limits of reason, isn't as directly tied to the emotional and existential emphasis that characterized the beginnings of Romanticism as Rousseau's ideas.\n\nConsidering the options and the context of the quote, Rousseau's emphasis on the corruption of natural goodness by society, his elevation of sentiment, and his critique of artificial constructs align most closely with questioning reliance on pure reason in favor of more emotive and natural responses, which were foundational to the Romantic movement. The quote's horror at natural destruction and human suffering also reflects the Romantic fascination with the powerful forces of nature and the depths of human emotion, often contrasted with the perceived coldness of purely rational systems.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 471,
        "output_tokens": 805,
        "total_tokens": 1276
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4498",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"His Majesty the Emperor of China agrees, that British subjects, with their families and establishments, shall be allowed to reside, for the purposes of carrying on their mercantile pursuits, without molestation or restraint, at the cities and towns of Canton, Amoy, Foochowfoo, Ningpo, and Shanghai; and Her Majesty the Queen of Great Britain, &c., will appoint Superintendents, or Consular officers, to reside at each of the above-named cities or towns, to be the medium of communication between the Chinese authorities and the said merchants, and to see that the just duties and other dues of the Chinese Government, as hereafter provided for, are duly discharged by Her Britannic Majesty's subjects.\"\nTreaty of Nanjing, 1839\nThe concessions offered in this treaty most directly created British\n\nOptions: \nA. colonies\nB. diplomatic embassies\nC. outposts\nD. vassal states\nE. militarized zones\nF. dominions\nG. trading companies\nH. protectorates\nI. spheres of influence\nJ. businesses\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
