    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4611",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"I travelled thence to 'Aden, the port of Yemen, on the coast of the ocean. It is surrounded by mountains and can be approached from one side only; it has no crops, trees, or water, but has reservoirs in which rainwater is collected. The Arabs often cut off the inhabitants from their supply of drinking-water until the they buy them off with money and pieces of cloth. It is an exceedingly hot place. It is the port of the Indians, and to it come large vessels from Kinbayat [Cambay], Kawlam [Quilon], Calicut and many other Malabar ports [on the south-west coast of India]. There are Indian merchants living there, as well as Egyptian merchants. Its inhabitants are all either merchants, porters, or fishermen. Some of the merchants are immensely rich, so rich that sometimes a single merchant is sole owner of a large ship with all it contains, and this is a subject of ostentation and rivalry amongst them. In spite of that they are pious, humble, upright, and generous in character, treat strangers well, give liberally to devotees, and pay in full the tithes due to God.\"\nIbn Battuta, ca. 1325–1354 C.E.\nIbn Battuta's experiences were LEAST similar to the experiences of which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. Lorenzo de Medici\nB. Sir Francis Drake\nC. Hernán Cortés\nD. Zheng He\nE. Vasco da Gama\nF. Christopher Columbus\nG. Xuanzang\nH. Marco Polo\nI. Ferdinand Magellan\nJ. John Cabot\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The provided text describes Ibn Battuta's travels and observations of Aden. The question asks which individual's experiences were LEAST similar to Ibn Battuta's. Ibn Battuta was a religious scholar and prolific traveler who documented his journeys for religious and cultural understanding. His travels were focused on religious pilgrimage, trade, and observing different societies.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n\n*   **A. Lorenzo de Medici:** A Florentine statesman and patron of the arts. While a prominent figure during the Renaissance, his experiences were primarily political and cultural within Italy, not extensive global travel for documentation.\n*   **B. Sir Francis Drake:** An English explorer, privateer, and admiral. He circumnavigated the globe, engaged in naval warfare, and plundered Spanish ships. His activities were largely military and acquisitive, unlike Ibn Battuta's scholarly and observational approach.\n*   **C. Hernán Cortés:** A Spanish conquistador who led the expedition that caused the fall of the Aztec Empire. His experiences were driven by conquest and colonization, very different from Ibn Battuta's.\n*   **D. Zheng He:** A Chinese admiral and explorer during the Ming Dynasty. He led large naval expeditions across the Indian Ocean, primarily for diplomatic and trade purposes, and documenting the regions visited. This has similarities to Ibn Battuta's extensive travel and observation of trade and cultures.\n*   **E. Vasco da Gama:** A Portuguese explorer and the first European to reach India by sea. His primary goal was to establish a sea route for trade, which involved navigating and interacting with the same regions Ibn Battuta visited.\n*   **F. Christopher Columbus:** An Italian explorer who sailed for Spain, aiming to reach Asia by sailing west. His voyages led to the European discovery of the Americas, and his motivations were exploration and trade, though his impact was colonization.\n*   **G. Xuanzang:** A Chinese Buddhist monk who traveled to India to collect Buddhist scriptures. His journey was primarily a religious pilgrimage and scholarly pursuit, remarkably similar to aspects of Ibn Battuta's travels.\n*   **H. Marco Polo:** A Venetian merchant, explorer, and writer who traveled to Asia. His detailed accounts of his travels in China and Southeast Asia are comparable to Ibn Battuta's observational style.\n*   **I. Ferdinand Magellan:** A Portuguese explorer who led the first circumnavigation of the Earth. His voyage was about exploration and discovery, charting new maritime routes.\n*   **J. John Cabot:** An Italian explorer and navigator who sailed for England, reaching North America. His expeditions were focused on exploration and claiming territory for England.\n\nThe experiences of **Hernán Cortés** were the LEAST similar. Ibn Battuta was a scholar and traveler documenting societies and religious practices. Cortés was a conquistador whose primary aim was conquest and subjugation, a stark contrast to Ibn Battuta's observational and diplomatic approach.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 402,
        "output_tokens": 607,
        "total_tokens": 1009
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4614",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What distinguishes the trade patterns of the Mesolithic from those of the Upper Paleolithic?\n\nOptions: \nA. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled less far and fewer goods were exchanged\nB. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled farther but the same number of goods were exchanged\nC. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled less far but the same number of goods were exchanged\nD. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled the same distance but more goods were exchanged\nE. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled farther but fewer goods were exchanged\nF. in the Upper Paleolithic, goods traveled less far but more goods were exchanged\nG. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled the same distance but fewer goods were exchanged\nH. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled less far but more goods were exchanged\nI. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled farther and more goods were exchanged\nJ. in the Mesolithic, goods traveled the same distance and the same number of goods were exchanged\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Mesolithic period, following the Upper Paleolithic, saw significant changes in human societies. Archaeological evidence suggests a general trend of increasing regionalism in resource use during the Mesolithic. This meant that communities relied more on local resources, leading to shorter distances for the movement of goods. While there was still exchange, the scale and distances involved were often reduced compared to the more widespread networks of the Upper Paleolithic.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is **A**.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 259,
        "output_tokens": 92,
        "total_tokens": 351
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
