    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4617",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Based on the morphology of fossil hominids dating from about 1.8 million until about 400,000 years ago, researchers argue that:\n\nOptions: \nA. There is no evidence of separate hominid species during this period\nB. hominids dating from this time can be grouped into different species in Africa, Asia, and Europe.\nC. The hominids from this period are not related to Homo sapiens\nD. There were two or more unrelated and geographically widespread hominid species during this period.\nE. Hominids during this period evolved from earlier Asian hominids, not African\nF. All hominids during this period can be grouped into a single species known as Homo habilis\nG. Only one single hominid species existed during this period and evolved into Homo sapiens\nH. there were two or more related but geographically separate hominid species during this period that evolved from earlier African hominids\nI. all of the above\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks about the morphological evidence for hominid species between 1.8 million and 400,000 years ago. This period encompasses species like Homo erectus and potentially early Homo heidelbergensis, which were geographically widespread. The diversity of fossils found across Africa, Asia, and Europe suggests the presence of different species. Option B accurately reflects this evidence by grouping hominids from this time into different species found in various continents. Options A, C, D, E, F, and G are contradicted by the fossil record of this era, which shows distinct species and African origins for later hominids. Option H suggests related but separate species and African origins, which is also plausible but option B is more direct in stating the geographical distribution of distinct species, which is a primary observation from morphology. Considering the broad temporal and geographical scope, option B aligns best with the general understanding of hominid evolution during this period based on morphology.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
