      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{C}. The language of protest used by Calhoun in his \"Exposition and Protest\" reflects a similar defiance against government overreach as that of the American colonists in their response to the British Stamp Act of 1765. Both contexts highlight a situation where the entities involved felt their rights and sovereignty were being infringed upon by a higher authority, leading to a collective expression of dissent.",
