    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4691",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The Chinese are all infidels: they worship images, and burn their dead just like the [Hindus]. The King of China is a Tartar, and one of the descendants of [Ghenghis] Khan…In all the Chinese provinces, there is a town for the [Muslims], and in this they reside. They also have cells, colleges, and mosques, and are made much of by the Kings of China…\nWhen we approached this city [of Hangzhou] we were met by its judge, the [elders] of Islamism, and the great merchants. The [Muslims] are exceedingly numerous here. This whole city is surrounded by a wall: each of the six [districts of Hangzhou] is also surrounded by a wall. In the first reside the guards, with their commander. I was told that, in the muster-rolls, these amount to twelve thousand…In the second division are the Jews, Christians, and the Turks who worship the sun: these are numerous, their number is not known: and theirs is the most beautiful city. Their streets are well disposed, and their great men are exceedingly wealthy. There are in the city a great number of [Muslims], with some of whom I resided for fifteen days; and was treated most [honorably]…\"\nIbn Battuta, Voyages, 1332–1346 C.E.\nWhich of the following conclusions about the period 600 C.E.–1450 C.E. is most directly supported by the passage?\n\nOptions: \nA. Technological developments were hindered due to religious beliefs.\nB. Technological developments spread across trade routes.\nC. The empires of steppe nomads united trade links across Eurasia.\nD. Religious tolerance was not practiced in major empires.\nE. New religious traditions overturned prior lasting religious beliefs.\nF. The Chinese empire was isolated and had little contact with other civilizations.\nG. Trade was primarily conducted within individual civilizations and not between them.\nH. The spread of Islam was primarily through military conquest.\nI. The Chinese empire was primarily Jewish and Christian during this period.\nJ. Long-distance contact between civilizations stagnated.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage describes the presence of Muslims, Jews, Christians, and Turks (who worship the sun) in Hangzhou, and their favorable reception by the Chinese rulers. This directly contradicts the idea of religious intolerance. Options A, B, C, E, G, H, I, and J are not directly supported or are contradicted by the text. Option F is also contradicted as the text shows significant contact. Therefore, the passage directly supports the conclusion that religious tolerance was practiced in this major Chinese city.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
